            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary consensus views clean and unclean as representing moral purity and holiness versus sin and corruption, with Old Testament ritual laws typifying this spiritual reality. Alternatives in the consensus recognize the original category as ritual fitness for sacred space, distinct from moral guilt.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Defines clean as being free from defilement and fit for permitted contact, emphasizing that uncleanness can be physical, ceremonial, or moral. It explicitly notes that uncleanness does not always imply guilt and must be distinguished from sanctification (holiness).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Moral purity, holiness, and the forgiveness of sin, or typological ritual fitness.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Freedom from defilement and fitness for permitted contact or use, encompassing physical, ceremonial, and moral dimensions without inherently implying guilt or holiness.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the common practice of collapsing cleanliness into moral purity and holiness. It insists on maintaining the distinction between being clean (free from defilement) and being sanctified (holy claim and use), and clarifies that uncleanness does not necessitate guilt.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book does not contradict the existence of moral cleansing, but it denies the consensus's primary tendency to equate cleanliness with holiness and uncleanness with sinfulness.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By drawing on the academically recognized cultic distinctions (which appear as alternatives in the consensus), the book refines the mainstream definition. It elevates 'fitness for contact' to the core definition and systematically untangles cleanliness from sanctification and guilt across all biblical contexts.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While the Old Testament meticulously distinguishes cleanliness from holiness and guilt (as supported by Leviticus 10:10 and the cited purity laws), the New Testament often uses cleansing and sanctification in parallel or interchangeably (e.g., Ephesians 5:26), which may justify the consensus's conflation in a Christian theological context.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
